The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
atomic = xe_pagefault_access_is_atomic(pf->consumer.access_type);
if (xe_vma_is_cpu_addr_mirror(vma))
    err = xe_svm_handle_pagefault(vm, vma, gt, pf->consumer.page_addr, atomic);
else
    err = xe_pagefault_handle_vma(gt, vma, atomic);
```
Reason: After finding the VMA, the original code directly proceeded to determine the access type (atomic or not) and invoke the appropriate handler without checking whether the VMA is read-only. This allowed write or atomic access to be passed to the VMA even if it was read-only, enabling writes to read-only memory regions.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (xe_vma_read_only(vma) &&
    pf->consumer.access_type != XE_PAGEFAULT_ACCESS_TYPE_READ) {
    err = -EPERM;
    goto unlock_vm;
}
```
Reason: This new check rejects any non-read access (write or atomic) to a read-only VMA by returning an error (-EPERM) before the handler is called, preventing writes to read-only memory regions.

